Now let's write the bitonic sort kernel. Bitonic sorting involves comparator networks, and we will have three nested loops: one for each phase, one for each sub-phase, and one for the actual comparisons. We will write a kernel that takes the matrix data, the current iteration, and a flag indicating the direction of sorting (ascending or descending).

Here is the kernel code:

```c
__global__ void bitonicSortKernel(int *mat, int iteration, bool dir)
{
    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    int offset = idx * COLS;
    int i, j, k;
    int temp;

    for (i = 1 << (iteration - 1); i > 0; i = i >> 1)
    {
        for (j = i; j > 0; j = j >> 1)
        {
            for (k = 0; k < (COLS >> 1); ++k)
            {
                int ixj = k ^ j;
                if ((k & i) == 0 && (ixj > k) && (dir == (mat[offset + k] > mat[offset + ixj])))
                {
                    // Swap elements
                    temp = mat[offset + k];
                    mat[offset + k] = mat[offset + ixj];
                    mat[offset + ixj] = temp;
                }
            }
            __syncthreads();
        }
    }
}
```

