It appears that you are applying bitonic sort only to individual sequences throughout the iterations, but not across the sequence boundaries. Due to this, each sequence is sorted individually, but the overall array is not sorted completely.

To fix this issue, you can change the bitonic sort implementation to support multiple sequences and make sure it sorts them as a single global sequence.

Here is the modified version of the bitonic_sort function to achieve this:

```cpp
void bitonic_sort(int *values, int sequence_length, int num_sequences) {
  for (int stage = 0; stage < (int)log2f(sequence_length); ++stage) {
    for (int step = sequence_length / 2; step > 0; step /= 2) {
      unsigned int numBlocks = (sequence_length * num_sequences + BLOCK_SIZE - 1) / BLOCK_SIZE; // Update number of blocks based on the number of sequences
      bitonic_sort_step<<<numBlocks, BLOCK_SIZE>>>(values, stage, step);
      cudaDeviceSynchronize();
    }
  }
  for (int stage = 0; stage < (int)log2f(num_sequences); ++stage) {
    for (int step = num_sequences / 2; step > 0; step /= 2) {
      unsigned int numBlocks = (sequence_length * num_sequences + BLOCK_SIZE - 1) / BLOCK_SIZE; // Update number of blocks based on the number of sequences
      bitonic_merge<<<numBlocks, BLOCK_SIZE>>>(values, stage, step);
      cudaDeviceSynchronize();
    }
  }
}
