To write an API in C# for a MongoDB database, you will need to:

1. Install the necessary NuGet packages
2. Set up a MongoDB connection
3. Create a model (class) that represents the data structure you are working with
4. Create a Data Access Layer (Repository) to interact with the MongoDB database
5. Implement an API controller that calls the repository methods to complete various CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations

Here's a step-by-step guide with explanations and code samples:

1. Install necessary NuGet packages
- In your C# project, you will need to install the following NuGet packages:
  - MongoDB.Driver
  - Swashbuckle.AspNetCore (for Swagger API documentation)

You can install them using Visual Studio's NuGet Package Manager or by running the following commands in the Package Manager Console:

```
Install-Package MongoDB.Driver
Install-Package Swashbuckle.AspNetCore
```

2. Set up MongoDB connection
- In your `appsettings.json` file, add your MongoDB connection string and database name:

```json
{
  "MongoDB": {
    "ConnectionString": "your_connection_string_here",
    "DatabaseName": "your_database_name_here"
  },
  // other settings...
}
```

- In your `Startup.cs` file, inject the MongoDB settings into the IConfiguration:

```csharp
public Startup(IConfiguration configuration)
{
  Configuration = configuration;
}

public IConfiguration Configuration { get; }
```

3. Create a model
- I'll assume you have a collection called `products` in the MongoDB database:

```csharp
public class Product
{
  [BsonId]
  [BsonRepresentation(BsonType.ObjectId)]
  public string Id { get; set; }

  [BsonElement("name")]
  public string Name { get; set; }

  [BsonElement("category")]
  public string Category { get; set; }

  [BsonElement("price")]
  public decimal Price { get; set; }
}
```

4. Create Data Access Layer (Repository)

```csharp
public interface IProductRepository
{
  Task<IEnumerable<Product>> GetProductsAsync();
  Task<Product> GetProductByIdAsync(string id);
  Task CreateProductAsync(Product product);
  Task<bool> UpdateProductAsync(Product product);
  Task<bool> DeleteProductAsync(string id);
}

public class ProductRepository : IProductRepository
{
  private readonly IMongoCollection<Product> _products;

  public ProductRepository(IConfiguration config)
  {
    var client = new MongoClient(config.GetSection("MongoDB:ConnectionString").Value);
    var database = client.GetDatabase(config.GetSection("MongoDB:DatabaseName").Value);
    _products = database.GetCollection<Product>("products");
  }

  // Implement the CRUD methods using _products collection
}
```

5. Implement API controller

```csharp
[Route("api/[controller]")]
[ApiController]
public class ProductsController : ControllerBase
{
  private readonly IProductRepository _productRepository;

  public ProductsController(IProductRepository productRepository)
  {
    _productRepository = productRepository;
  }

  // Implement the API methods using _productRepository
}
```

Now you can implement the CRUD methods in the controller and repository layers and test the API using a tool such as Postman or Swagger.

