To display a slice of a 3D image in a 2D image viewer with the ActiViz library (vtk), you'll need to do the following:

1. Load the 3D image using vtkImageReader.
2. Use vtkImageReslice to extract the 2D slice from the 3D image along a specific plane.
3. Use vtkImageActor and vtkRenderer to display the extracted 2D slice in a render window.

Here's an example on how to display a 2D slice from a 3D image:

```csharp
using Kitware.VTK;

vtkImageReader reader = vtkImageReader.New();
// Load your 3D image file, for example
reader.SetFileName("path/to/your/image.vtk");
reader.Update();

// Find image dimensions
int[] imageDims = reader.GetOutput().GetDimensions(); // imageDims is an array with [x, y, z] dimensions

// Create an image reslice object
vtkImageReslice reslice = vtkImageReslice.New();
reslice.SetInputConnection(reader.GetOutputPort());

// Set the slicing plane: for example, extract the middle slice in XY plane
double[] center = new double[3];
center[0] = imageDims[0] / 2.0;
center[1] = imageDims[1] / 2.0;
center[2] = imageDims[2] / 2.0;

// Set the reslicing transform. In this case, we just extract a slice in the XY plane
reslice.SetResliceAxesDirectionCosines(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1);
reslice.SetResliceAxesOrigin(center);

// Use vtkExtractEdges to find the edges of the image
vtkExtractEdges edges = vtkExtractEdges.New();
edges.SetInputConnection(reslice.GetOutputPort());
edges.Update();

// Create an image actor to display the extracted slice
vtkImageActor imageActor = vtkImageActor.New();
imageActor.SetInput(reslice.GetOutput());

// Create a renderer and add the image actor
vtkRenderer renderer = vtkRenderer.New();
renderer.AddActor(imageActor);
renderer.ResetCamera();

// Create a render window
vtkRenderWindow renderWindow = vtkRenderWindow.New();
renderWindow.AddRenderer(renderer);

// Create an interactor for the render window
vtkRenderWindowInteractor interactor = vtkRenderWindowInteractor.New();
interactor.SetRenderWindow(renderWindow);
interactor.Initialize();
```

Here, we're using the vtkImageReslice class to extract a slice of the image and then vtkImageActor and vtkRenderer to display the 2D slice in a render window. The example extracts the middle slice in the XY plane, but you can adjust the `SetResliceAxesDirectionCosines()` and `SetResliceAxesOrigin()` parameters to change the slicing plane and position.

Using vtkFlyingEdges3D, you can obtain a polygonal representation of the bone or skin by specifying an appropriate isosurface value (i.e., the threshold level) in your 3D image. The threshold value for bone or skin heavily depends on the range of values used in your dataset and how image intensity represents the tissues.

Here's an example of extracting the bone or skin using vtkFlyingEdges3D:

```csharp
using Kitware.VTK;

// Read your 3D image.
vtkImageReader reader = vtkImageReader.New();
reader.SetFileName("path/to/your/image.vtk");
reader.Update();

// Set the appropriate threshold value for bone or skin.
// For example, if your dataset has intensity values ranging from 0 - 255:
// 100 may represent the skin threshold, while 200 may represent the bone threshold.
float threshold = 200; // Adjust this value according to your dataset.

// Create a vtkFlyingEdges3D object.
vtkFlyingEdges3D flyingEdges = vtkFlyingEdges3D.New();
flyingEdges.SetInputConnection(reader.GetOutputPort());
flyingEdges.SetValue(0, threshold); // Set the isosurface value (threshold level).
flyingEdges.ComputeNormalsOn();

// Create a vtkPolyDataMapper and set the input as the output from vtkFlyingEdges3D.
vtkPolyDataMapper mapper = vtkPolyDataMapper.New();
mapper.SetInputConnection(flyingEdges.GetOutputPort());
mapper.ScalarVisibilityOff(); // Don't use scalars for coloring.

// Create an actor.
vtkActor actor = vtkActor.New();
actor.SetMapper(mapper);

// Create a renderer and add the actor.
vtkRenderer renderer = vtkRenderer.New();
renderer.AddActor(actor);
renderer.ResetCamera();

// Create a render window.
vtkRenderWindow renderWindow = vtkRenderWindow.New();
renderWindow.AddRenderer(renderer);

// Create an interactor and set the render window.
vtkRenderWindowInteractor interactor = vtkRenderWindowInteractor.New();
interactor.SetRenderWindow(renderWindow);
interactor.Initialize();

// Start the interaction.
interactor.Start();
```
In this example, we first read a 3D image using vtkImageReader. Make sure to adjust the input file path and select the appropriate reader according to your dataset. Next, we define the threshold value corresponding to bone or skin. This value depends on your specific dataset.

We then create a vtkFlyingEdges3D object and set the input as the output from the image reader. We use the `SetValue` method to set the isosurface value to the threshold level. Then, we create a vtkPolyDataMapper for mapping the output polygonal data from vtkFlyingEdges3D and use a vtkActor to render it. Finally, we create a vtkRenderer, vtkRenderWindow, and vtkRenderWindowInteractor to display the extracted bone or skin surface.

