To display a slice of a 3D image using SetDisplayExtent, you can follow these steps:

1. Load the 3D image using vtkImageReader.
2. Use vtkImageActor and SetDisplayExtent to display the specific slice from the 3D image along a specific plane.
3. Use vtkRenderer to render the extracted 2D slice in a render window.

Here's an example to display a 2D slice from a 3D image using SetDisplayExtent:

```csharp
using Kitware.VTK;

// Load your 3D image file.
vtkImageReader reader = vtkImageReader.New();
reader.SetFileName("path/to/your/image.vtk");
reader.Update();

// Find the image dimensions.
int[] imageDims = reader.GetOutput().GetDimensions();

// Set the slicing plane and index: For example, extract the middle slice in XY plane.
int planeIndex = imageDims[2] / 2;

// Create an image actor.
vtkImageActor imageActor = vtkImageActor.New();
imageActor.GetMapper().SetInputConnection(reader.GetOutputPort());

// SetDisplayExtent can be used to control the slice displayed by vtkImageActor.
// The arguments for SetDisplayExtent are: (xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax, zmin, zmax)
imageActor.SetDisplayExtent(0, imageDims[0] - 1, 0, imageDims[1] - 1, planeIndex, planeIndex); // This will display only the middle slice in the XY plane.

// Create a renderer and add the image actor.
vtkRenderer renderer = vtkRenderer.New();
renderer.AddActor(imageActor);
renderer.ResetCamera();

// Create a render window.
vtkRenderWindow renderWindow = vtkRenderWindow.New();
renderWindow.AddRenderer(renderer);

// Create an interactor for the render window.
vtkRenderWindowInteractor interactor = vtkRenderWindowInteractor.New();
interactor.SetRenderWindow(renderWindow);
interactor.Initialize();

// Start the interaction.
interactor.Start();
```

In this example, after reading the 3D image and getting its dimensions, we create a vtkImageActor. Instead of using vtkImageReslice, we configure the image actor to display only one slice in the desired plane. For this, we use SetDisplayExtent, providing the slice number (planeIndex) for the minimum and maximum z values. This way, the image actor displays only the specific slice in the XY plane. Finally, we create a vtkRenderer, vtkRenderWindow, and vtkRenderWindowInteractor to render the slice on the screen.

To set the vtkCamera for rendering the sliced images in the correct orientation for Axial (XY), Sagittal (YZ), and Coronal (XZ) views, you need to configure the camera's position, focal point, and view up directions according to each view.

Here's an example that demonstrates how to set the vtkCamera for correct orientation of the 3 different views:

```csharp
using Kitware.VTK;

// Function to create a reslice with SetDisplayExtent
Func<vtkImageData, int, int[], vtkImageActor> createResliceWithDisplayExtent = (vtkImageData imageData, int sliceIndex, int[] extent) =>
{
    vtkImageActor imageActor = vtkImageActor.New();
    imageActor.GetMapper().SetInputData(imageData);
    imageActor.SetDisplayExtent(extent[0], extent[1], extent[2], extent[3], sliceIndex, sliceIndex);
    return imageActor;
};

vtkImageReader reader = vtkImageReader.New();
reader.SetFileName("path/to/your/image.vtk");
reader.Update();

int[] imageDims = reader.GetOutput().GetDimensions();
int planeIndex = imageDims[2] / 2;
int coronalPlane = imageDims[1] / 2;
int sagittalPlane = imageDims[0] / 2;

// Create image actors for each view using set display extent
vtkImageActor axialActor = createResliceWithDisplayExtent(reader.GetOutput(), planeIndex, new[] { 0, imageDims[0] - 1, 0, imageDims[1] - 1 }); // Axial (XY)
vtkImageActor sagittalActor = createResliceWithDisplayExtent(reader.GetOutput(), sagittalPlane, new[] { 0, imageDims[2] - 1, 0, imageDims[1] - 1 }); // Sagittal (YZ)
vtkImageActor coronalActor = createResliceWithDisplayExtent(reader.GetOutput(), coronalPlane, new[] { 0, imageDims[0] - 1, 0, imageDims[2] - 1 }); // Coronal (XZ)

vtkRenderer renderer = vtkRenderer.New();

// Add actors for each view
renderer.AddActor(axialActor);
renderer.AddActor(sagittalActor);
renderer.AddActor(coronalActor);

// Set the position of each actor to display side by side
axialActor.SetPosition(0, 0, 0);
sagittalActor.SetPosition(imageDims[0] * 1.2, 0, 0); // Place sagittal next to axial
coronalActor.SetPosition(imageDims[0] * 2.4, 0, 0); // Place coronal next to sagittal

vtkRenderWindow renderWindow = vtkRenderWindow.New();
renderWindow.AddRenderer(renderer);
renderWindow.SetSize(800, 800); // Adjust the window size considering all views

vtkCamera camera = renderer.GetActiveCamera();

// Set the camera focal point at the center of the volume
double[] center = new double[3];
center[0] = imageDims[0] / 2.0;
center[1] = imageDims[1] / 2.0;
center[2] = imageDims[2] / 2.0;
camera.SetFocalPoint(center[0], center[1], center[2]);

// Set the camera position, view up direction, and parallel scale for each view
// Axial view
camera.SetPosition(center[0], center[1], center[2] * 2);
camera.SetViewUp(0, 1, 0);
camera.SetViewAngle(30);
renderer.ResetCamera();
double parallelScale = camera.GetParallelScale();

// Sagittal view
camera.SetPosition(center[0] * 2.2, center[1], center[2]);
camera.SetViewUp(0, 1, 0);
camera.SetParallelScale(parallelScale);

// Coronal view
camera.SetPosition(center[0], center[1] * -2.2, center[2]);
camera.SetViewUp(0, 0, 1);
camera.SetParallelScale(parallelScale);

vtkRenderWindowInteractor interactor = vtkRenderWindowInteractor.New();
interactor.SetRenderWindow(renderWindow);
interactor.Initialize();
interactor.Start();
```

In this example, we create a separate vtkImageActor for each view (axial, sagittal, and coronal) using the `createResliceWithDisplayExtent` function. We add these image actors to the same renderer. Next, we set the position of each actor to display the views side by side.

To configure the vtkCamera, we first set the camera's focal point at the center of the volume. Then, we set the camera position, view up direction, and parallel scale for each view. For the axial view, the camera position is along the Z-axis, for the sagittal view, it is along the X-axis, and for the coronal view, it is along the Y-axis with a negative sign. The view up direction of the camera is set to represent the orientation of each slice in the correct order. Finally, the vtkRenderWindow and vtkRenderWindowInteractor are used to display the slices of the three views.

