To obtain the geometrical center of slot vertices in Spine 2D using the latest C++ runtime API, you can follow these steps:

1. Include the necessary Spine runtime headers:

```cpp
#include <spine/spine.h>
#include <spine/AnimationState.h>
#include <spine/Bone.h>
#include <spine/Skeleton.h>
#include <spine/Slot.h>
```

2. Obtain a reference to the Spine skeleton and find the slot you want to process:

```cpp
spine::Skeleton *skeleton = // retrieve the skeleton from your Spine object
spine::Slot *slot = skeleton->findSlot("your_slot_name");
```

3. Calculate the geometrical center of the vertices of the slot's attachment (assuming it's a region attachment):

```cpp
if (slot && slot->getAttachment()) {
  const spine::Vector<float> &vertices = static_cast<spine::RegionAttachment *>(slot->getAttachment())->getVertices();

  float centerX = 0.0f;
  float centerY = 0.0f;

  for (size_t i = 0; i < vertices.size(); i += 2) {
    centerX += vertices[i];
    centerY += vertices[i + 1];
  }

  centerX /= (vertices.size() / 2);
  centerY /= (vertices.size() / 2);

  // centerX and centerY now contain the geometrical center of the slot vertices
}
```

Note: This code snippet assumes that the attachment of the slot is a `RegionAttachment`. If you have a different attachment type like `MeshAttachment`, you need to adapt the code accordingly.

