To compare two lists with different objects in C#, you can use LINQ to project the two lists into a common type before you compare them. This involves combining the unique properties of each type into a new type with only the common properties, which can then be used for comparison.

Here's an example:

```csharp
class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
}

class Employee
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
    public decimal Salary { get; set; }
}

var persons = new List<Person>
{
    new Person { Name = "John", Age = 30 },
    new Person { Name = "Jane", Age = 25 },
    new Person { Name = "Bob", Age = 32 }
};

var employees = new List<Employee>
{
    new Employee { Name = "John", Age = 30, Salary = 5000 },
    new Employee { Name = "Mary", Age = 28, Salary = 8000 }
};

var commonTypeList = persons.Select(p => new { p.Name, p.Age })
                            .Union(employees.Select(e => new { e.Name, e.Age }))
                            .ToList();

var distinctCommonItems = commonTypeList.Distinct().ToList();
```

In this example, we create two lists of `Person` and `Employee` objects with different properties. We then use LINQ's `Select` method to project each list into a list of anonymous objects with only the `Name` and `Age` properties:

```csharp
persons.Select(p => new { p.Name, p.Age }) // project Person list into anonymous type with Name and Age properties
employees.Select(e => new { e.Name, e.Age }) // project Employee list into anonymous type with Name and Age properties
```

We then use the `Union` method to combine the two anonymous object lists into a single list of anonymous objects with only the `Name` and `Age` properties. This `commonTypeList` list will contain both `Person` and `Employee` objects.

Finally, we use the `Distinct` method to find the distinct anonymous objects by their `Name` and `Age` values. The resulting list, `distinctCommonItems`, will contain only the unique instances of anonymous objects that exist in both `Person` and `Employee` lists.

