Unfortunately, it's not possible to automatically grant permission for a Google Sheets script to pull data from another spreadsheet due to security reasons. Google Script runs with the permissions of the user, and this action requires the user to manually authorize the permissions.

However, you can mitigate the issue by prompting users to grant permission on their first run. You can use a custom menu or a button that triggers a function to authorize permission.

Here's an example which uses a custom menu to run the function that triggers permission request:

1. Go to the "List" spreadsheet.
2. Select Extensions > Apps Script. A new Google Sheets Script editor will open.
3. Create a new script, and paste the following code:

```javascript
function onOpen() {
  var spreadsheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActive();
  var menuItems = [
    {name: 'Grant Permission', functionName: 'requestPermission'}
  ];
  spreadsheet.addMenu('Custom Menu', menuItems);
}

function requestPermission() {
  // Change this to the ID of the source spreadsheet you want to grant permission to
  var sourceSpreadsheetId = "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx";

  // Open the source spreadsheet to force permission request
  var sourceSpreadsheet = SpreadsheetApp.openById(sourceSpreadsheetId);

  if (sourceSpreadsheet != null) {
    SpreadsheetApp.getUi().alert('Permission has been granted successfully!');
  } else {
    SpreadsheetApp.getUi().alert('Error: Unable to grant permission. Please check the source spreadsheet id.');
  }
}
```

4. Replace "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx" with the ID of the source spreadsheet you are trying to grant permission to.
5. Save the script
6. Now, when users open the "List" spreadsheet, they will see a custom menu called "Custom Menu" with an option called "Grant Permission". When they click on it, it will prompt them to grant permission to the source spreadsheet.

Remember that each user needs to grant permission for their own account.

