Based on the provided requirements, I have made changes to the draw function and applied the z-index factor and round gradient to the grid points. Here's the updated draw function:

```javascript
function draw() {
  ctx.fillStyle = "rgba(1,2,1,0.8)";
  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

  // Sort points based on z-index to draw them in the correct order
  const sortedGrid = grid.sort((a, b) => {
    const rotatedA = rotate(a, angleX, angleY);
    const rotatedB = rotate(b, angleX, angleY);
    return rotatedB.z - rotatedA.z;
  });

  for (const point of sortedGrid) {
    const rotated = rotate(point, angleX, angleY);
    const projected = project(rotated, canvas.width, canvas.height);

    // Apply round gradient
    const gradient = ctx.createRadialGradient(projected.x, projected.y, 0, projected.x, projected.y, 5);
    gradient.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(55,155,255,0.8)');
    gradient.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(55,155,255,0.2)');
    ctx.fillStyle = gradient;

    const size = 2 * (zoom / (zoom + rotated.z));
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(projected.x, projected.y, size, 0, Math.PI * 2); 
    ctx.closePath();
    ctx.fill();
  }

  // Drawing clicked points in green
  ctx.fillStyle = "rgba(255,200,50,0.8)";
  for (const clickedPoint of clickedPoints) {
    const rotated = rotate(clickedPoint, angleX, angleY);
    const projected = project(rotated, canvas.width, canvas.height);

    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(projected.x, projected.y, 4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.closePath();
    ctx.fill();
  }

  for (const line of lines) {
    drawLine(line);
  }

  if (activeLine !== null) {
    drawLine(activeLine);
  }

  angleX += rotationSpeed;
  angleY += rotationSpeed;

  requestAnimationFrame(draw);
}

draw();
```

This new code sorts the grid points based on their z-index, ensuring that points further away in the z-axis are drawn first, thereby being hidden by points closer to the viewer. Additionally, each grid point will have a round gradient applied, creating a more volumetric appearance. Finally, the grid points' sizes are now determined by the z-index, making them smaller when they are further away from the viewer.

First, you need to create the custom table models. Let's assume you want to create tables for `Blog` and `Post` with a one-to-many relationship. Here are the models:

1. Create a new folder called "Models" in your project, if it's not already there.

2. Create a new C# class file called "Blog.cs" inside the "Models" folder and add the following code:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations;
using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations.Schema;

namespace YourAppNamespace.Models
{
    public class Blog
    {
        [Key]
        public int BlogId { get; set; }
        [Required]
        public string Name { get; set; }
        public DateTime Created { get; set; }

        public ICollection<Post> Posts { get; set; }
    }
}
```

3. Create another new C# class file called "Post.cs" inside the "Models" folder and add the following code:

```csharp
using System;
using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations;
using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations.Schema;

namespace YourAppNamespace.Models
{
    public class Post
    {
        [Key]
        public int PostId { get; set; }
        [Required]
        public string Title { get; set; }
        public string Content { get; set; }
        public DateTime Posted { get; set; }

        [ForeignKey("BlogId")]
        public int BlogId { get; set; }
        public Blog Blog { get; set; }
    }
}
```

Now, add the `DbSet` properties for the new tables to your application's `DbContext` class. By default, it should be located in the `Data` folder and named `ApplicationDbContext.cs`. 

4. Open the `ApplicationDbContext.cs` file, add the following properties, and make sure to include the reference to the models namespace:

```csharp
using YourAppNamespace.Models;

public class ApplicationDbContext : IdentityDbContext
{
    // Add these DbSet properties
    public DbSet<Blog> Blogs { get; set; }
    public DbSet<Post> Posts { get; set; }

    // The rest of your ApplicationDbContext class ...
}
```

Finally, you will need to create and apply a migration to create the tables in the database.

5. Open a terminal/command prompt, navigate to your project directory and run the following commands:

```bash
dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate
dotnet ef database update
```

This will create a new migration named "InitialCreate" and apply it, creating the tables in the database. Note that it requires the "Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" NuGet package. If you don't have it, you can install it by running:

```bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design
```

