- It defines the `fetchAndLoadCSV` function, which fetches the CSV file using the `fetch` function, and converts the response to text. Then it calls the `csvToOptions` function to create the option elements and adds them to the select element.

4. Finally, run the HTML file in your browser, and you should see the content of the CSV file as options in the select list. Note that fetching local files may not work with some browsers due to security restrictions. You may need to run your code on a local or remote web server.

To convert images to WebP format in an ASP.NET Core MVC application, you'll first need to install the `ImageSharp.Web` NuGet package, which provides the functionality for processing images in various formats including WebP.

Install the package by running the following command in your terminal:

```
dotnet add package SixLabors.ImageSharp.Web
```

Once the package is installed, you can use it to convert images to WebP format. Here's an example of how to implement an image upload action in your MVC controller that accepts PNG or JPEG images and saves them as WebP:

```csharp
using System.IO;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using SixLabors.ImageSharp;
using SixLabors.ImageSharp.Processing;

// ...

[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> UploadImage(IFormFile file)
{
    if (file == null || file.Length == 0)
    {
        return BadRequest("File not selected or empty.");
    }

    // Check if the file is a PNG or JPEG image
    var contentType = file.ContentType.ToLower();
    if (contentType != "image/png" && contentType != "image/jpeg")
    {
        return BadRequest("Invalid file format. Please upload a PNG or JPEG image.");
    }

    // Set the target directory for saving images
    var targetDirectory = Path.Combine("wwwroot", "images", "uploads");

    // Create the directory if it does not exist
    if (!Directory.Exists(targetDirectory))
    {
        Directory.CreateDirectory(targetDirectory);
    }

    // Generate a unique filename with a .webp extension
    var newFilename = Guid.NewGuid().ToString() + ".webp";
    var targetPath = Path.Combine(targetDirectory, newFilename);

    using (var fileStream = new FileStream(targetPath, FileMode.Create))
    {
        // Load the image from the uploaded file
        using (var image = await Image.LoadAsync(file.OpenReadStream()))
        {
            // Save the image as a WebP file
            await image.SaveAsync(fileStream, new SixLabors.ImageSharp.Formats.WebP.WebPEncoder());
        }
    }

    // The image has been saved as a WebP file. You can return the filename or a URL to display it, for example.
    var savedImageUrl = "/images/uploads/" + newFilename;
    return Ok(savedImageUrl);
}
```

This code assumes the existence of an HTML form in your view with an `<input type="file">` field for uploading images. The action reads the uploaded file, checks if it's a PNG or JPEG image, and then converts and saves it as a WebP file. Note that this example stores the images in a `wwwroot/images/uploads` folder inside your application; ensure you modify the paths according to your desired folder structure.

That's it! Simply add the provided code into one of your controllers and configure the frontend to handle the image upload, and your application will automatically convert uploaded PNG/JPEG images to WebP format.

