To create a custom table and add it to the default DbContext in an ASP.NET Core MVC project, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. Create a new model class for the custom table.
2. Add a DbSet property representing the table to your ApplicationDbContext class.
3. Create and execute a migration to update the database for the new table.

Assuming you have an ASP.NET Core MVC project, here's an example to do that:

Step 1: Create a custom model class.

In the Models folder, create a new class named CustomTable.cs:

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations;

namespace YourNamespace.Models
{
    public class CustomTable
    {
        [Key]
        public int Id { get; set; }
        public string Name { get; set; }
        public int Value { get; set; }
    }
}
```

Step 2: Add the DbSet property representing the table to your ApplicationDbContext.

Open your ApplicationDbContext.cs file, include the new model in the using statements, and add a DbSet property for the custom table:

```csharp
using YourNamespace.Models; // Add the namespace for CustomTable
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

namespace YourNamespace.Data
{
    public class ApplicationDbContext : IdentityDbContext
    {
        public ApplicationDbContext(DbContextOptions<ApplicationDbContext> options) : base(options) { }

        public DbSet<CustomTable> CustomTables { get; set; } // Add this line

        protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder builder)
        {
            base.OnModelCreating(builder);
            // Your code to customize the model
        }
    }
}
```

Step 3: Creating and executing the migration to update the database for the new table.

In your terminal or Package Manager Console, navigate to your project directory, and run the following command to create a migration:

```sh
dotnet ef migrations add AddCustomTable
```

Or in Package Manager Console:

```sh
Add-Migration AddCustomTable
```

Next, execute the migration to apply the changes to the database:

```sh
dotnet ef database update
```

Or in Package Manager Console:

```sh
Update-Database
```

That's it! The custom table is now added to the default DbContext in your ASP.NET Core MVC project.

