The error `ReferenceError: role is not defined` is occuring in the `search` function. Below is the corrected `search` function. I've replaced `role` with `hiddenRole`.

Also, I moved the filtering by `role` outside the second `if` statement to ensure that it filters the musicians with roles even if there is no `query`.

```javascript
function search(query, hiddenRole) {
  const data = fs.readFileSync('./db/musicians.json');
  const musicians = JSON.parse(data).musicians.map(musician => {
    return {
      name: musician.name,
      genre: musician.genre,
      originalName: musician.name,
      profileLink: `/profile/${musician.id}`,
      thumbnail: musician.thumbnail,
      soundcloud: musician.soundcloud,
      role: musician.role
    };
  });

  let results = musicians.filter(musician => hiddenRole === '' || musician.role === hiddenRole);

  if (query) {
    const lowerQuery = query.toLowerCase();
    results = results.filter(musician => {
      const nameScore = musician.name.toLowerCase().startsWith(lowerQuery) ? 2 : musician.name.toLowerCase().includes(lowerQuery) ? 1 : 0;
      const genreScore = musician.genre.toLowerCase().startsWith(lowerQuery) ? 2 : musician.genre.toLowerCase().includes(lowerQuery) ? 1 : 0;
      return nameScore + genreScore > 0;
    }).sort((a, b) => {
      const aNameScore = a.name.toLowerCase().startsWith(lowerQuery) ? 2 : a.name.toLowerCase().includes(lowerQuery) ? 1 : 0;
      const bNameScore = b.name.toLowerCase().startsWith(lowerQuery) ? 2 : b.name.toLowerCase().includes(lowerQuery) ? 1 : 0;
      const aGenreScore = a.genre.toLowerCase().startsWith(lowerQuery) ? 2 : a.genre.toLowerCase().includes(lowerQuery) ? 1 : 0;
      const bGenreScore = b.genre.toLowerCase().startsWith(lowerQuery) ? 2 : b.genre.toLowerCase().includes(lowerQuery) ? 1 : 0;
      return (bNameScore + bGenreScore) - (aNameScore + aGenreScore);
    });
  }
  return results;
}
```

This should fix the issues you were experiencing and make the search work as intended.

Sure, here's an example serverless Lambda project written in Node.js that syncs data between two Trello cards on two different boards:

1. Create a new Serverless Framework project:
   * Install the Serverless Framework by running `$ npm install -g serverless`.
   * Create a new Serverless Framework project by running `$ serverless create --template aws-nodejs --path my-project`.
   * Change into your project directory by running `$ cd my-project`.

2. Install the necessary dependencies:
   * Install the `trello` and `node-fetch` packages by running `$ npm install trello node-fetch`.

3. Create a Trello authentication credentials file:
   * Create a `credentials.json` file in your project directory.
   * Paste in the following JSON data and replace the placeholders with your Trello API key and token:

   ```
   {
     "key": "<your Trello API key>",
     "token": "<your Trello API token>"
   }
   ```

   * Save and close the file.

4. Define the Lambda function code:
   * Create a new file named `handler.js` in your project directory.
   * Paste in the following code:

   ```js
   const fetch = require("node-fetch");
   const Trello = require("trello");
   const credentials = require("./credentials.json");

   const TARGET_LIST_ID = "<target list ID>";

   const targetBoard = new Trello(credentials.key, credentials.token).setBoard("<target board ID>");
   const targetList = targetBoard.lists.get(TARGET_LIST_ID);

   module.exports.syncCards = async (event) => {
     // Extract data from the event object
     const { sourceCardListId, sourceCardId } = JSON.parse(event.body).action.data;

     // Retrieve the source card's data from the Trello API
     const response = await fetch(`https://api.trello.com/1/cards/${sourceCardId}?key=${credentials.key}&token=${credentials.token}`);
     const sourceCardData = await response.json();

     // Create a new card on the target board and list
     const cardData = {
       name: sourceCardData.name,
       desc: sourceCardData.desc,
       idMembers: sourceCardData.idMembers,
       idLabels: sourceCardData.idLabels,
     };
     targetList.cards.create(cardData);

     return {
       statusCode: 200,
       body: JSON.stringify({ success: true }),
     };
   };
   ```

   This function uses the `node-fetch` package to make HTTP requests to the Trello API, and the `trello` package to interact with Trello boards, lists, and cards. The `TARGET_LIST_ID` constant specifies the ID of the list on the target board where new cards should be created. The `targetBoard` and `targetList` constants specify the target board and list objects.

   The `syncCards` function is the main Lambda function that will be triggered by a Trello webhook. It extracts the necessary data from the webhook event, retrieves the source card's data from the Trello API, creates a new card on the target board and list, and returns a success response.

5. Configure the Trello webhook:
   * Log in to your Trello account and navigate to the board that contains the source card.
   * Click the "Show Menu" button at the top right of the screen.
   * Click "More" and then "Settings".
   * Scroll down to the "Webhooks" section and click "Create New Webhook".
   * Enter a name for your webhook.
   * Enter the URL of your Lambda function, provided by the Serverless Framework.
   * Choose "All" for the "Trigger" field.
   * Click "Create Webhook".

6. Deploy your Lambda function:
   * Deploy your function to AWS Lambda by running `$ serverless deploy`.

That's it! You've created a serverless Lambda function that can be triggered by a Trello webhook and syncs data between two Trello cards on two different boards.

