Creating a complete Java-based website to read and interpret tarot cards would require significant development time and effort. However, I can provide you with a basic blueprint of how you could structure your project, and the main classes you may want to include in your Java web application.

First, you will need to create a new Java project, preferably using a web framework like JavaServer Faces (JSF), JavaServer Pages (JSP), or Spring Boot. These frameworks have built-in libraries and tools to build web applications efficiently.

Here's a simple structure for your Java classes:

1. TarotCard.java: This class will represent a Tarot card.

```java
public class TarotCard {
    private String name;
    private String imageName;
    private String description;
    // Other related properties, like meanings for the card in different positions.

    public TarotCard(String name, String imageName, String description) {
        this.name = name;
        this.imageName = imageName;
        this.description = description;
    }

    // Getters and setters
}
```
2. TarotDeck.java: This class will represent the Tarot deck and provide methods to initialize and shuffle the deck.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class TarotDeck {
    private List<TarotCard> deck;

    public TarotDeck() {
        deck = new ArrayList<>();
        initializeDeck(); // Fill the deck with Tarot cards.
        Collections.shuffle(deck);
    }

    private void initializeDeck() {
        // Add cards to the deck.
    }

    public TarotCard drawCard() {
        return deck.get(0);
    }

    public void shuffleDeck(){
        Collections.shuffle(deck);
    }
}
```

3. TarotReading.java: This class will handle the Tarot reading process and layout.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class TarotReading {
    private TarotDeck deck;
    private List<TarotCard> spread;

    public TarotReading() {
        deck = new TarotDeck();
        spread = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public List<TarotCard> performReading(int numberOfCards) {
        spread.clear();

        for (int i = 0; i < numberOfCards; i++) {
            spread.add(deck.drawCard());
        }

        return spread;
    }
}
```
