```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i, j, k;
    char str[12];

    for(i = 'a'; i <= 'z'; i++) {
        for(j = 'a'; j <= 'z'; j++) {
            for(k = 0; k < 10; k++) {
                sprintf(str, "%c%c%010d", i, j, k);
                printf("%s\n", str);
            }
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code uses three nested loops to iterate over all the possible combinations of letters and digits. The `sprintf` function is used to format the string with the current values of `i`, `j`, and `k`. The resulting string is then printed to the console. The output will start with "aa000000000", followed by "aa000000001", "aa000000002", and so on, up to "zzzzzzzzzzzz".

Here's an example C++ code that generates the same string as the previous C code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>

int main() {
    std::stringstream ss;

    for (char i = 'a'; i <= 'z'; i++) {
        for (char j = 'a'; j <= 'z'; j++) {
            for (int k = 0; k < 10000000000; k++) {
                ss << i << j << std::setw(10) << std::setfill('0') << k << '\n';
                std::cout << ss.str();
                ss.str("");
            }
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code uses the `stringstream` class to build the string and formatted text output. The `setw` and `setfill` functions from the `iomanip` library are used to ensure that `k` is formatted with leading zeroes. Output is printed to the console within the inner loop and the `stringstream` object is cleared after each iteration. The output will start with "aa000000000", followed by "aa000000001", "aa000000002", and so on, up to "zzzzzzzzzzzz".

