To avoid panic in a proc macro, you can use `syn::Error::to_compile_error` to create a custom error message that will be displayed during compilation.

The following example demonstrates how to handle and report a syntax error in a proc macro using the `syn` crate:

```rust
// Import syn and proc_macro crates
use proc_macro::TokenStream;
use syn::{parse_macro_input, DeriveInput};

#[proc_macro_derive(YourDerive)]
pub fn your_derive(item: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let input = parse_macro_input!(item as DeriveInput);

    let result = process_derive_input(&input);

    match result {
        Ok(tokens) => tokens.into(),
        Err(error) => error.to_compile_error().into(),
    }
}

fn process_derive_input(input: &DeriveInput) -> Result<proc_macro2::TokenStream, syn::Error> {
    // Process the input, and if there is a syntax error (or any other error),
    // you can use `syn::Error::new` with the `Span` of the problematic token

    // For example, if you find an unexpected closing delimiter token:
    let unexpected_closing_delimiter = syn::Error::new(proc_macro2::Span::call_site(), "Unexpected closing delimiter");

    Err(unexpected_closing_delimiter)
}
```

In the above example, the `process_derive_input` function simulates a case where a syntax error (unexpected closing delimiter) has been encountered. Instead of panicking, you can use `syn::Error::new` to create a custom error with a suitable message that will be reported during compilation.

This way, if the proc macro encounters a syntax error, it doesn't panic, but instead, it returns a meaningful error message.

To handle errors in a procedural macro without using external crates like `syn`, you can use the built-in `proc_macro` and `proc_macro2` crates' error handling capabilities. For instance, you can use the `proc_macro2::Span` to create a diagnostic error message.

Here's an example of how to handle syntax errors without the `syn` crate:

```rust
// Import proc_macro and proc_macro2 Crate
use proc_macro::{TokenStream};
use proc_macro2::{Span, TokenStream as TokenStream2};

#[proc_macro]
pub fn your_macro(item: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let input: TokenStream2 = item.into();

    let result = process_macro_input(&input);

    match result {
        Ok(tokens) => tokens.into(),
        Err(error) => {
            // Use the `proc_macro::Span` to create a diagnostic error message.
            let mut diagnostic = error.span.unwrap().unwrap().error(error.msg);
            if let Some(note) = error.note {
                diagnostic = diagnostic.help(note);
            }

            diagnostic.emit();
            TokenStream::new()
        },
    }
}

struct MacroError {
    span: Option<Span>,
    msg: String,
    note: Option<String>,
}

fn process_macro_input(input: &TokenStream2) -> Result<TokenStream2, MacroError> {
    // Process the input, and if there is a syntax error (or any other error),
    // you can create a custom error message

    // You would typically analyze `input` by parsing and searching for specific tokens or elements

    // For example, you would find an unexpected closing delimiter token and its span:
    let span = proc_macro2::Span::call_site();
    let error_msg = "Unexpected closing delimiter";
    let error_note = Some("Please fix the syntax error");

    let macro_error = MacroError {
        span: Some(span),
        msg: error_msg.to_string(),
        note: error_note.map(ToString::to_string),
    };

    Err(macro_error)
}
```

This example defines a `MacroError` struct that contains an error message and an associated span. When an error is encountered, you can create a custom error message using a `MacroError` instance, and then use `proc_macro::Span` to emit the error as a diagnostic during compilation.

In order to demonstrate error checking in the `process_macro_input` function, I'll show you a simple macro example that parses input for parentheses and generates tokens based on a custom rule. If there are unbalanced or misplaced parentheses, an error is reported.

```rust
use proc_macro::TokenStream;
use proc_macro2::{Delimiter, Group, Span, TokenTree, TokenStream as TokenStream2};

#[proc_macro]
pub fn your_macro(item: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let input: TokenStream2 = item.into();

    let result = process_macro_input(&input);

    match result {
        Ok(tokens) => tokens.into(),
        Err(error) => {
            let mut diagnostic = error.span.unwrap().unwrap().error(error.msg);
            if let Some(note) = error.note {
                diagnostic = diagnostic.help(note);
            }

            diagnostic.emit();
            TokenStream::new()
        },
    }
}

struct MacroError {
    span: Option<Span>,
    msg: String,
    note: Option<String>,
}

fn process_macro_input(input: &TokenStream2) -> Result<TokenStream2, MacroError> {
    let mut input_iter = input.clone().into_iter();
    let mut output = TokenStream2::new();

    let mut open_paren_count = 0;

    while let Some(token) = input_iter.next() {
        match &token {
            TokenTree::Group(group) if group.delimiter() == Delimiter::Parenthesis => {
                open_paren_count += 1;

                let tokens_inside_paren = group.stream();
                let tokens_inside_paren_count = tokens_inside_paren.clone().into_iter().count();

                if tokens_inside_paren_count > 0 {
                    return Err(MacroError {
                        span: Some(token.span()),
                        msg: "Unexpected tokens inside parentheses".to_string(),
                        note: Some("Please fix the syntax error".to_string()),
                    });
                }
            },
            TokenTree::Punct(ref punct) if punct.as_char() == ')' => {
                if open_paren_count == 0 {
                    return Err(MacroError{
                        span: Some(token.span()),
                        msg: "Unexpected closing parenthesis".to_string(),
                        note: Some("Please fix the syntax error".to_string()),
                    });
                }
                open_paren_count -= 1;
                output.extend(std::iter::repeat(token.clone()).take(2));
            },
            token => {
                output.extend(std::iter::repeat(token.clone()).take(1));
            },
        }
    }

    if open_paren_count != 0 {
        return Err(MacroError {
            span: Some(Span::call_site()),
            msg: "Unbalanced parentheses".to_string(),
            note: Some("Please fix the syntax error".to_string()),
        });
    }

    Ok(output)
}
```

This example implements a simple macro that validates the input syntax by checking for balanced parentheses and ensuring there are no tokens inside it. If it encounters misplaced or unbalanced parentheses, it reports an error message with the corresponding span.

The `process_macro_input` function iterates over the tokens in the input stream and generates new tokens based on the custom rule. If it encounters an unbalanced parenthesis or tokens inside a parenthesis, it creates a `MacroError` instance and returns an error.

Here's a modified version of the `process_macro_input` function that checks for _any_ syntax error. It doesn't specify the exact type of error, but simply reports an error if something is considered invalid according to the custom rule:

```rust
use proc_macro::TokenStream;
use proc_macro2::{Delimiter, Group, Span, TokenTree, TokenStream as TokenStream2};

#[proc_macro]
pub fn your_macro(item: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let input: TokenStream2 = item.into();

    let result = process_macro_input(&input);

    match result {
        Ok(tokens) => tokens.into(),
        Err(error) => {
            let mut diagnostic = error.span.unwrap().unwrap().error(error.msg);
            if let Some(note) = error.note {
                diagnostic = diagnostic.help(note);
            }

            diagnostic.emit();
            TokenStream::new()
        },
    }
}

struct MacroError {
    span: Option<Span>,
    msg: String,
    note: Option<String>,
}

fn process_macro_input(input: &TokenStream2) -> Result<TokenStream2, MacroError> {
    let mut input_iter = input.clone().into_iter();
    let mut output = TokenStream2::new();

    let mut open_paren_count = 0;
    let mut has_error = false;

    while let Some(token) = input_iter.next() {
        match &token {
            TokenTree::Group(group) if group.delimiter() == Delimiter::Parenthesis => {
                open_paren_count += 1;

                let tokens_inside_paren = group.stream();
                let tokens_inside_paren_count = tokens_inside_paren.clone().into_iter().count();

                if tokens_inside_paren_count > 0 {
                    has_error = true;
                }
            },
            TokenTree::Punct(ref punct) if punct.as_char() == ')' => {
                if open_paren_count == 0 {
                    has_error = true;
                } else {
                    open_paren_count -= 1;
                }
            },
            _ => (),
        }
    }

    if open_paren_count != 0 || has_error {
        return Err(MacroError {
            span: Some(Span::call_site()),
            msg: "Invalid syntax".to_string(),
            note: Some("Please fix the input".to_string()),
        });
    }

    Ok(input.clone())
}
```

In this version, the `process_macro_input` function does not report a specific error and only reports "Invalid syntax." It sets a flag (`has_error`) whenever a mismatched parenthesis, unexpected tokens inside parentheses, or any other issue occurs. If the `has_error` flag is set or the parentheses are unbalanced, it reports that the input is invalid but doesn't provide additional details.

