To create a 5x5 grid inventory with each grid being 100px and centering the entire inventory, you can use either CSS Grid or Flexbox along with Svelte. In this example, I will use CSS Grid.

First, create a new Svelte component called `Inventory.svelte` and add the following code:

```html
<script>
  let items = Array.from({ length: 25 }, (_, i) => ({ id: i + 1 }));
</script>

<style>
  .container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 100px);
    grid-template-rows: repeat(5, 100px);
    gap: 4px;
    justify-content: center;
    margin: auto;
    width: 520px;
  }
  .item {
    background-color: #f1f1f1;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    font-size: 20px;
  }
</style>

<div class="container">
  {#each items as { id } (id)}
    <div class="item">{id}</div>
  {/each}
</div>
```

Now, include the `Inventory.svelte` component inside your main Svelte component (e.g., `App.svelte`):

```html
<script>
  import Inventory from "./Inventory.svelte";
</script>

<Inventory />
```

The `Inventory.svelte` component creates a 5x5 grid and centers the inventory using a combination of CSS Grid and auto-margins. Each item has a size of 100x100px, and I've used the Svelte `{#each}` loop to create 25 items for demonstration purposes.

Assuming you have a table (an array) of items, you can use the Svelte `{#each}` directive to loop through the items and render them in the grid inventory. Here's an example:

Let's say your items are represented as an array of objects with an `id` and a `name`, like this:

```javascript
const items = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Item 1' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Item 2' },
  { id: 3, name: 'Item 3' },
  // ...
];
```

You can modify your `Inventory.svelte` component to accept this `items` array as a prop and use `{#each}` to render the items:

```html
<!-- Inventory.svelte -->
<script>
  export let items = [];
</script>

<style>
  .container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 100px);
    grid-template-rows: repeat(5, 100px);
    gap: 4px;
    justify-content: center;
    margin: auto;
    width: 520px;
  }
  .item {
    background-color: #f1f1f1;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    font-size: 20px;
  }
</style>

<div class="container">
  {#each items as item (item.id)}
    <div class="item">{item.name}</div>
  {/each}
</div>
```

Now, you can pass the `items` array to the `Inventory.svelte` component in your main Svelte component (e.g., `App.svelte`):

```html
<script>
  import Inventory from "./Inventory.svelte";

  const items = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Item 1' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Item 2' },
    { id: 3, name: 'Item 3' },
    // ...
  ];
</script>

<Inventory {items} />
```

This will render the names of the items in the grid. You can customize the rendering of each item by modifying the contents of the `item` div inside the `{#each}` loop in the `Inventory.svelte` component.

To center the inventory both horizontally and vertically, you can use Flexbox in a wrapper element. Here's how you can modify your `Inventory.svelte` component:

```html
<!-- Inventory.svelte -->
<script>
  export let items = [];
</script>

<style>
  .wrapper {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 100vh;
  }
  .container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 100px);
    grid-template-rows: repeat(5, 100px);
    gap: 4px;
  }
  .item {
    background-color: #f1f1f1;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    font-size: 20px;
    height: 100px;
    width: 100px;
  }
</style>

<div class="wrapper">
  <div class="container">
    {#each items as item (item.id)}
      <div class="item">{item.name}</div>
    {/each}
  </div>
</div>
```

I added a wrapper div with the class `wrapper` that uses Flexbox to center its content both horizontally and vertically. The `min-height: 100vh;` property ensures that the wrapper takes at least the full viewport height. Inside the wrapper, the container div keeps its CSS Grid behavior. This will center the inventory container within the viewport.

