Solution 1: Using `syn::parse_str`

This solution uses syn crate to parse the string into an expression.

```rust
use proc_macro2::TokenStream;
use syn;

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    match syn::parse_str::<syn::Expr>(stream) {
        Ok(expr) => expr.to_token_stream(),
        Err(_) => TokenStream::new(),
    }
}
```

Solution 2: Using lazy_static

This solution uses lazy_static to memoize the results of parsing the string into a TokenStream or an empty TokenStream if the string is erroneous.

```rust
use proc_macro2::TokenStream;
use once_cell::sync::Lazy;
use syn;

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    static CACHE: Lazy<std::collections::HashMap<String, TokenStream>> = Lazy::new(|| {
        std::collections::HashMap::new()
    });

    let cache = CACHE.lock().unwrap();
    cache.get(stream).cloned().unwrap_or_else(|| {
        let tokens = match syn::parse_str::<syn::Expr>(stream) {
            Ok(expr) => expr.to_token_stream(),
            Err(_) => TokenStream::new(),
        };
        cache.insert(stream.to_string(), tokens.clone());
        tokens
    })
}
```

Solution 3: Using `quote::quote_spanned`

The following solution uses `quote::quote_spanned` to produce an empty error span.

```rust
use proc_macro2::TokenStream;
use syn;

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    match syn::parse_str::<syn::Expr>(stream) {
        Ok(expr) => expr.to_token_stream(),
        Err(e) => {
            let sp = e.span();
            quote::quote_spanned! { sp => }
        }
    }
}
```

Solution 4: Using `syn::Expr`

In this solution, we use `syn::Expr` to parse the string and handle errors directly with syn error type.

```rust
use proc_macro2::TokenStream;
use syn;

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    syn::parse_str::<syn::Expr>(stream).unwrap_or_else(|_| TokenStream::new())
}
```

Solution 5: Using `proc_macro2::Group`

We can use `proc_macro2::Group` to parse the string into a TokenStream and handle the error by returning an empty TokenStream.

```rust
use proc_macro2::{Delimiter, Group, TokenStream};

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    let tokens = Group::new(Delimiter::Brace, stream.parse().unwrap_or_default());
    tokens.stream()
}
```

Solution 6: Matching on token kind

The solution is to directly match on the parsed tokens and return an empty TokenStream if there's an error.

```rust
use proc_macro2::{TokenStream, TokenTree};
use syn;

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    let tokens = stream.parse::<TokenStream>().unwrap_or_default();

    if tokens.is_empty() {
        TokenStream::new()
    } else {
        match tokens.into_iter().next().unwrap() {
            TokenTree::Ident(_) | TokenTree::Literal(_) | TokenTree::Group(_) => tokens,
            _ => TokenStream::new(),
        }
    }
}
```

Solution 7: Function-like Macro

If you don't mind changing the usage and can deal with a macro invocation, you can use a function-like macro.

```rust
use proc_macro2::TokenStream;

macro_rules! tokens_from_str {
    ($stream:expr) => {{
        match syn::parse_str::<syn::Expr>($stream) {
            Ok(expr) => expr.to_token_stream(),
            Err(_) => TokenStream::new(),
        }
    }};
}

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    let _ = tokens_from_str!(erroneous_expr_str);
}
```

Solution 8: Custom Error Reporting

This solution shows how to handle custom spans for erroneous expressions.

```rust
use proc_macro2::{TokenStream, TokenTree};
use syn::{self, Error};

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    let tokens = match stream.parse::<TokenStream>() {
        Ok(ts) => ts,
        Err(_) => {
            // Set custom_span as the span of the error
            let custom_span = proc_macro2::Span::call_site(); // or any other span
            return Error::new(custom_span, "custom error message").to_compile_error();
        }
    };

    match tokens.into_iter().next().unwrap() {
        TokenTree::Ident(_) | TokenTree::Literal(_) | TokenTree::Group(_) => tokens.clone(),
        _ => TokenStream::new(),
    }
}
```

Solution 9: Using `syn::parse::<syn::Expr>()` directly

An alternative way is to use the `parse` function with the desired parser directly.

```rust
use proc_macro2::TokenStream;
use syn;

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    match syn::parse_str::<syn::Expr>(stream) {
        Ok(expr) => expr.to_token_stream(),
        Err(_) => TokenStream::new(),
    }
}
```

