To achieve the desired behavior, you can change the `format` function to handle the single backtick (for bold text) and triple backtick (for code blocks) cases separately. I have modified the `format` function below:

```javascript
function format(str) {
  const code_block_regex = /(```(\w*)\n[\s\S]*?```)/g;
  const inline_code_regex = /(`[^`\n]+`)/g;

  // Replace triple backtick code blocks
  str = str.replace(code_block_regex, (match, p1, p2) => {
    const language = p2 || 'undefined';
    const code = match.substring(3 + p2.length, match.length - 3).trim();
    return `<pre><code class="hljs language-${language}">${code}</code></pre>`;
  });

  // Replace single backtick inline code with bold text
  str = str.replace(inline_code_regex, (match) => {
    const text = match.substring(1, match.length - 1);
    return `<b>${text}</b>`;
  });

  return str;
}
```

Use this modified `format` function in your existing code to format the user's messages and GPT messages. This function will replace triple backtick code blocks with the appropriate `<pre><code>` tags and replace single backtick inline code with bold text.

With the changes applied above, the text inside single backticks will appear as bold, and the code blocks with triple backticks will remain unchanged.

