To achieve this, you can modify the `format()` function to check for the type of code block (one backtick or three backticks) and apply the necessary formatting.  I've added a new `formatCodeBlock()` function inside the existing `format()` function to handle the processing of the code blocks.

Here's an updated version of your `format()` function to implement the desired behavior for one backtick and three backticks:

```javascript
function format(text) {
  const formatCodeBlock = (code, lang) => {
    if (lang) {
      return `<code class="${lang.trim()} hljs">${code}</code>`;
    }
    return `<code class="hljs language-undefined"><b>${code}</b></code>`;
  };

  text = text.replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;');

  const regex = /(`{1,3})(.+?)(\1)/gs;
  let match;
  let formattedText = '';
  let lastIndex = 0;

  while ((match = regex.exec(text)) !== null) {
    formattedText += text.slice(lastIndex, match.index);
    const code = match[2].trim();
    const formatting = match[1].length;

    if (formatting === 3) {
      // For three backticks
      const lang = code.startsWith('css') ? 'language-css' : undefined;
      const codeText = code.startsWith('css') ? code.slice(3).trim() : code;
      formattedText += formatCodeBlock(codeText, lang);
    } else {
      // For one backtick
      formattedText += formatCodeBlock(code);
    }

    lastIndex = regex.lastIndex;
  }
  formattedText += text.slice(lastIndex);
  return formattedText;
}
```

This version of `format()` checks if there are one or three backticks and formats the code block accordingly. If there are three backticks and a language identifier (e.g., 'css'), it will apply the corresponding class (e.g., 'language-css), otherwise, the class will be 'hljs language-undefined'. For one backtick, the text inside the code block will be made bold.

