Here's an example code on how you can make requests using axios and async.queue in Node.js:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');
const async = require('async');

// Create a queue with concurrency of 5
const queue = async.queue(async (url) => {
  const response = await axios.get(url);
  console.log(`${url} - Status code: ${response.status}`);
}, 5);

// Add some URLs to the queue
queue.push('https://www.google.com');
queue.push('https://www.yahoo.com');
queue.push('https://www.bing.com');
queue.push('https://www.github.com');

// Drain event will be triggered when all tasks are completed
queue.drain(() => {
  console.log('All requests completed');
});
```

In this example, we import the `axios` and `async` modules, create an async queue with a concurrency of 5, and add some URLs to it. 

Then, we define the function that will be executed by each task in the queue. It receives a URL as a parameter and makes a GET request using `axios`. Once the response is received, we print the URL and the HTTP status code to the console.

Finally, we listen to the `drain` event of the queue, which is emitted when all tasks have been completed. In our case, we use it to print a message to the console indicating that all requests have been completed.

To return the responses, you can modify the function executed by each task in the queue to return the response data instead of printing it to the console. You can then use a callback function to capture the response data and do something with it.

Here's an example code:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');
const async = require('async');

// Create a queue with concurrency of 5
const queue = async.queue(async (url, callback) => {
  try {
    const response = await axios.get(url);
    callback(null, response.data); // Call the callback with the response data
  } catch(error) {
    callback(error); // Call the callback with the error object
  }
}, 5);

// Add some URLs to the queue
queue.push('https://www.google.com');
queue.push('https://www.yahoo.com');
queue.push('https://www.bing.com');
queue.push('https://www.github.com');

// Drain event will be triggered when all tasks are completed
queue.drain(() => {
  console.log('All requests completed');
});

// Listen to the completion of each task
queue.drain('taskComplete', (taskId, responseData) => {
  console.log(`Response data for task ${taskId}: ${responseData}`);
});

// Listen to the errors of each task
queue.drain('taskError', (taskId, error) => {
  console.log(`Error for task ${taskId}: ${error.message}`);
});
```

In this example, we modified the function executed by each task to call the `callback` function with the `response.data` object if the request was successful, or with the `error` object if it failed. We also added two event handlers to listen to the completion and errors of each task.

The `drain` event is triggered when all tasks have been completed. In our case, we use it to print a message to the console indicating that all requests have been completed.

The `taskComplete` event is triggered when a task is completed successfully. In our case, we use it to print the response data to the console along with the task ID.

The `taskError` event is triggered when a task encounters an error. In our case, we use it to print the error message to the console along with the task ID.

