Here is an example of how to create a timer interrupt in C that runs a function after a certain amount of time (in milliseconds):

```c
#include <avr/io.h>      // include AVR IO library
#include <avr/interrupt.h>      // include AVR interrupt library

volatile uint16_t timer_count = 0;  // initialize a global variable to store the timer count

void timer_setup() {
   TCCR0A = 0x00;   // Timer/Counter Control Register A, set to 0
   TCCR0B = (1 << CS01) | (1 << CS00); // set prescaler to 64 (f_CPU/64)
   OCR0A = 250;  // set output compare value (250*64/f_CPU gives interrupt every 1 ms)
   TIMSK0 = (1 << OCIE0A);  // enable interrupt on compare match
   sei();   // set the global interrupt enable bit
}

ISR(TIMER0_COMPA_vect) {
   timer_count++;   // increment the timer count on every interrupt
   if (timer_count >= x) {   // check if the desired time has elapsed
      myFunction();   // run the function if the time has elapsed
      timer_count = 0;   // reset the timer count
   }
}

void myFunction() {
   // do something
}

int main() {
   timer_setup();   // set up the timer interrupt
   while (1) {}   // infinite loop to keep the microcontroller running
   return 0;
}
```

In this example, the timer is set up to run at a frequency of 1 kHz (with a prescaler of 64) and to generate an interrupt on every compare match with a value of 250 (resulting in an interrupt every 1 ms). The ISR (interrupt service routine) increments the global variable `timer_count` on every interrupt and checks if the desired time `x` has elapsed. If it has, the function `myFunction()` is called and the timer count is reset to 0. The main loop of the program simply runs indefinitely to keep the microcontroller running.

Yes, in a Linux environment, you can use the `timer_create()` and `timer_settime()` functions to create a timer that runs a function after a certain amount of time. Here's an example:

```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <time.h>

timer_t timer;
int count = 0;

void myFunction(union sigval arg) {
    count++;
    printf("Timer count: %d\n", count);
    // do something here
}

int main() {
    struct sigevent sev;
    struct itimerspec its;

    // create the timer
    sev.sigev_notify = SIGEV_THREAD;
    sev.sigev_notify_function = myFunction;
    sev.sigev_notify_attributes = NULL;
    sev.sigev_value.sival_ptr = &timer;
    if (timer_create(CLOCK_REALTIME, &sev, &timer) != 0) {
        perror("timer_create");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // set the timer
    its.it_value.tv_sec = 1;  // initial delay
    its.it_value.tv_nsec = 0;
    its.it_interval.tv_sec = 1;  // interval for repeated timer
    its.it_interval.tv_nsec = 0;
    if (timer_settime(timer, 0, &its, NULL) != 0) {
        perror("timer_settime");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    while (1) {
        // do other stuff here
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `timer_create()` function is used to create the timer and specify the function to be called `myFunction()`. The `timer_settime()` function is used to set the timer with an initial delay of 1 second and an interval of 1 second for repeated timer. The `myFunction()` function is called every time the timer fires and `count` is incremented.

Note that in a Linux environment, you may need to link the `rt` library by adding `-lrt` at the end of the compile command.

In C, you can use the `clock_gettime()` function to get the system time in nanoseconds. Here's an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>

int main() {
    struct timespec t;

    if (clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &t) != 0) {  // get the system time
        perror("clock_gettime");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("System time: %ld s, %ld ns\n", t.tv_sec, t.tv_nsec);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `clock_gettime()` function is called with the `CLOCK_REALTIME` clock identifier to get the system time. The system time is returned in a `timespec` structure, which contains a `tv_sec` field representing whole seconds and a `tv_nsec` field representing nanoseconds within that second. The `printf()` function is used to print the system time in seconds and nanoseconds. Note that this will print the system time since January 1, 1970 (UNIX epoch).

To force a C code to start on even seconds, you can use the `clock_gettime()` function to get the current time and sleep until the next even second. Here's an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    struct timespec t;

    if (clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &t) != 0) {  // get the system time
        perror("clock_gettime");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Current time: %ld s, %ld ns\n", t.tv_sec, t.tv_nsec);

    if (t.tv_sec % 2 == 1) {  // check if the current time is odd
        printf("Sleeping until even second...\n");
        sleep(1 - (t.tv_nsec / 1000000000.0));  // sleep until next even second
    }

    printf("Starting C code...\n");
    // run your C code here

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `clock_gettime()` function is used to get the current time, and the `tv_sec` field is checked to see if it's odd. If it is, the program sleeps until the next even second by calculating the remaining time until the next second boundary with subtraction and then calling `sleep()` with that amount. If the current time is already even, the program continues immediately to run the C code. Note that this method is not guaranteed to start exactly on the even second due to the potential delay between getting the current time and sleeping.

To force a C code to start on whole seconds, you can use the `clock_gettime()` function to get the current time and sleep until the next whole second. Here's an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    struct timespec t;

    if (clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &t) != 0) {  // get the system time
        perror("clock_gettime");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Current time: %ld s, %ld ns\n", t.tv_sec, t.tv_nsec);

    if (t.tv_nsec > 0) {  // check if the current time is not on a whole second
        printf("Sleeping until next whole second...\n");
        sleep(1 - (t.tv_nsec / 1000000000.0));  // sleep until next whole second
    }

    printf("Starting C code...\n");
    // run your C code here

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `clock_gettime()` function is used to get the current time, and the `tv_nsec` field is checked to see if it's non-zero. If it is, the program sleeps until the next whole second by calculating the remaining time until the next second boundary with subtraction and then calling `sleep()` with that amount. If the current time is already on a whole second, the program continues immediately to run the C code. Note that this method is not guaranteed to start exactly on the whole second due to the potential delay between getting the current time and sleeping.

In C, when you divide an integer by an integer using the `/` operator, the result will always be an integer (the remainder is truncated). The same applies to floating-point division between two integers. This is called integer division.

However, when you divide a double by an integer, the result will be a double. In this case, the compiler first converts the integer value into a double and then performs the division, and the result is not truncated.

In the case of `x / 10`, if `x` is a double, it will be divided by the integer `10`. Since the "/" operator is associate left to right, it means the calculation will be evaluated from left to right, dividing x by 10, returning a double result value. However, if `x` is an integer, it will be divided by the integer `10`, and the result will be truncated to an integer. If you want the result to be a double, you can cast the integer to a double before the division like this:

```c
int x = 42;
double result = (double)x / 10;
```

This will first cast `x` to a double, and then perform the division, resulting in a double value. Alternatively, you can use the floating-point constant `10.0` instead of the integer `10`, which will cause the division to be performed using floating-point arithmetic:

```c
double x = 42.0;
double result = x / 10.0;
```

In standard C, the `sleep()` function can only be used to pause for a minimum of one second. In order to pause for a time less than one second, you can use the `nanosleep()` function, which allows for more precise timing. Here is an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>

int main() {
    struct timespec req, rem;
    req.tv_sec = 0;  // set seconds to 0
    req.tv_nsec = 500000000;  // set nanoseconds to 500 million (0.5 seconds)
    if (nanosleep(&req, &rem) != 0) {  // pause execution for 0.5 seconds
        printf("nanosleep interrupted: %ld s, %ld ns remaining\n", rem.tv_sec, rem.tv_nsec);
    }
    printf("Finished sleeping\n");
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, `struct timespec` is used to specify the duration of the pause in seconds and nanoseconds. The `nanosleep()` function call pauses execution of the program for the given duration and returns 0 if no errors occur. If `nanosleep()` is interrupted (e.g. by a signal), the remaining time left to sleep (in seconds and nanoseconds) is stored in the `rem` argument. 

Note that `nanosleep()` is a POSIX function and may not be available on all systems.

