Parsing command line arguments in C++ involves accessing the `argc` and `argv` parameters of the main function. `argc` represents the number of arguments passed and `argv` is an array of C-Strings containing the command-line arguments.

Here is an example program that takes two integer command-line arguments and prints their sum:

```c++
#include <iostream>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
  if(argc != 3) {
    std::cout << "Usage: ./program arg1 arg2" << std::endl;
    return 1;
  }

  int arg1 = std::stoi(argv[1]);
  int arg2 = std::stoi(argv[2]);

  std::cout << "Sum: " << arg1 + arg2 << std::endl;

  return 0;
}
```

This program first checks that exactly two arguments have been passed in. If not, it prints usage information and returns an error code. If the correct number of arguments is present, it uses `std::stoi` function to convert the C-Strings to integers, adds them together, and prints the result.

