In addition, the TypeScript version uses static typing throughout the code, which can help catch errors at compile time rather than at runtime. The JavaScript version does not have this feature, so errors may only be caught at runtime.

Overall, TypeScript adds additional features and syntax to JavaScript that can help make code more robust and easier to maintain, but it also requires additional setup and configuration.

