To achieve this functionality in Google Sheets using Google Apps Script's onEdit trigger, you can use the following code. This code will automatically insert a timestamp in the next column, whenever any data is changed in your specific column.

1. Open your Google Sheet.
2. Click on Extensions > Apps Script.
3. Delete any existing code, and paste the following code:

```javascript
function onEdit(e) {
  var sheet = e.source.getSheetByName('Sheet1'); // change 'Sheet1' to the name of your sheet
  var columnToWatch = 2; // change this to the number of the column you want to watch for changes (e.g., 1 for column A, 2 for column B, etc.)
  var columnForTimestamp = columnToWatch + 1; // timestamp will be inserted in the next column

  if (e.range.columnStart === columnToWatch) {
    var timestamp = new Date();
    sheet.getRange(e.range.rowStart, columnForTimestamp).setValue(timestamp);
  }
}
```

4. Change 'Sheet1' to the name of your sheet.
5. Change the value of `columnToWatch` to the column number (1 for column A, 2 for column B, etc.) you want to watch for changes.
6. Click on the save icon (floppy disk) to save the script.

Now, whenever you change any data in the specified column, a timestamp will be inserted in the next column.

