Creating a JMS consumer client with Weblogic and RabbitMQ in C# is possible, but it would involve several steps and configuration changes. I'll outline the general approach below, but please note that this solution might not be optimal because you'd be using JMS (Java Message Service) with C#, which isn't the most natural integration.

To do this, you will need the following:

1. RabbitMQ with the JMS topic exchange plugin installed.
2. Weblogic server with JMS resources (queue, connection factory) configured.
3. A C# client using a JMS compatible library like Apache NMS (recommended for .NET).
4. Configure a RabbitMQ JMS bridge in Weblogic to pass messages between the Weblogic JMS and RabbitMQ.

Here's a high-level overview of the steps:

1. Install and configure RabbitMQ with the JMS topic exchange plugin:

   Download the JMS Topic Exchange for RabbitMQ from here: https://github.com/simonmacmullen/rabbitmq-jms-topic-exchange and follow the installation instructions to enable the plugin on your RabbitMQ instance.

2. Configure Weblogic JMS resources:

   In the Weblogic server, create a JMS server, a JMS Module, a connection factory, and a queue. Configure the queue with the required JNDI name, and specify the appropriate URL to connect to RabbitMQ.

3. Create a C# client using Apache NMS:

   Use the Apache NMS library in the C# client to connect to the Weblogic server. Send and receive messages using this library. Here's a sample code for consuming messages from a queue:

```csharp
using Apache.NMS;
using Apache.NMS.Util;
using System;

namespace JMSConsumer
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Starting JMS consumer...");

            // set up the connection factory
            IConnectionFactory connectionFactory = new NMSConnectionFactory("jmsConnectionFactoryURL");

            using (IConnection connection = connectionFactory.CreateConnection())
            {
                using (ISession session = connection.CreateSession(AcknowledgementMode.AutoAcknowledge))
                {
                    // set up the destination (replace "queueName" with the appropriate queue)
                    IDestination destination = SessionUtil.GetDestination(session, "queue://queueName");

                    // set up the consumer
                    using (IMessageConsumer consumer = session.CreateConsumer(destination))
                    {
                        connection.Start();

                        IMessage message;
                        while ((message = consumer.Receive()) != null)
                        {
                            ITextMessage textMessage = message as ITextMessage;
                            if (textMessage != null)
                            {
                                Console.WriteLine("Message received: " + textMessage.Text);
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

4. Configure RabbitMQ-JMS bridge in Weblogic:

   Refer to the official Oracle documentation (https://docs.oracle.com/en/middleware/standalone/weblogic-server/14.1.1.0/wlach/oraclenetwork/RABMQ1-Oracle_WebLogic_Server.htm) to set up a JMS bridge. This will allow messages to be passed between the Weblogic JMS resources and RabbitMQ.

