Spring State Machine is a framework for creating state machines in Java with Spring. Here's how you can use Spring State Machine:

1. Add the Spring State Machine dependency to your project:

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.statemachine</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-statemachine-core</artifactId>
  <version>2.1.3.RELEASE</version>
</dependency>
```

2. Create a State Machine Configuration:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableStateMachine
public class StateMachineConfig extends StateMachineConfigurerAdapter<String, String> {

  @Override
  public void configure(StateMachineStateConfigurer<String, String> states) throws Exception {
    states.withStates()
        .initial("S1")
        .end("SF")
        .states(new HashSet<String>(Arrays.asList("S2", "S3")));
  }

  @Override
  public void configure(StateMachineTransitionConfigurer<String, String> transitions) throws Exception {
    transitions
        .withExternal()
            .source("S1")
            .target("S2")
            .event("E1")
            .and()
        .withExternal()
            .source("S2")
            .target("S3")
            .event("E2")
            .and()
        .withExternal()
            .source("S3")
            .target("SF")
            .event("E3");
  }
}
```

This configuration sets up a simple state machine with three states (`S1`, `S2`, and `S3`) and two events (`E1` and `E2`) triggering transitions between the states.

3. Create a State Machine:

```java
@Autowired
private StateMachine<String, String> stateMachine;

public void triggerEvent(String event) {
  stateMachine.sendEvent(event);
}
```

This code injects the `StateMachine` object created by Spring and provides a method for triggering an event.

4. Use the State Machine:

```java
