Is there a way to do this dynamically?

Yes, you can achieve this dynamically by creating a helper function template that combines component creation and loading in the `Factory` class. First, you need to modify the component classes to accept a construction from a JSON value, as shown below:

```cpp
struct Position {
    float x;
    float y;

    Position() = default;
    explicit Position(const rapidjson::Value& value)
        : x(value["x"].GetFloat()), y(value["y"].GetFloat()) {}
};

struct Name {
    std::string name;

    Name() = default;
    explicit Name(const rapidjson::Value& value) : name(value.GetString()) {}
};

struct Size {
    int width;
    int height;

    Size() = default;
    explicit Size(const rapidjson::Value& value)
        : width(value["width"].GetInt()), height(value["height"].GetInt()) {}
};

struct Velocity {
    int vel;

    Velocity() = default;
    explicit Velocity(const rapidjson::Value& value) : vel(value["vel"].GetInt()) {}
};
```

Then, in your `Factory` class, you can have a function template similar to the `CreateComponentFromJson`, like:

```cpp
template <typename T>
void LoadComponentData(Entity& entity, const rapidjson::Value& component)
{
    try
    {
        entity.AddComponent<T>({component});
    }
    catch (const std::runtime_error& e)
    {
        // You can handle exceptions or propagate them
        throw e;
    }
}
```

Now, let's update the `Scene::Load` function:

```cpp
void Scene::Load(const std::string& filename)
{
    // ... JSON parsing and error checking as before ...

    const rapidjson::Value& entitiesArray = document["Scene"]["entities"];

    // ... Initialize the Factory for component creation ...

    for (rapidjson::SizeType i = 0; i < entitiesArray.Size(); ++i)
    {
        const rapidjson::Value& entity = entitiesArray[i];

        // ... Create the new entity ...

        // Iterate through each component of the entity
        for (auto componentIter = entity.MemberBegin(); componentIter != entity.MemberEnd(); ++componentIter)
        {
            std::string componentName = componentIter->name.GetString();
            const rapidjson::Value& componentValue = componentIter->value;

            if (componentName == "Position")
            {
                factory.LoadComponentData<Position>(newEntity, componentValue);
            }
            else if (componentName == "Name")
            {
                factory.LoadComponentData<Name>(newEntity, componentValue);
            }
            else if (componentName == "Size")
            {
                factory.LoadComponentData<Size>(newEntity, componentValue);
            }
            else if (componentName == "Velocity")
            {
                factory.LoadComponentData<Velocity>(newEntity, componentValue);
            }
            else
            {
                // You can have a default case to handle other components
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this approach, the factory has a helper function `LoadComponentData` that creates and adds a component of a certain type to the entity, passing the relevant JSON value to the component's constructor. The `Scene::Load` function remains more or less the same, with the addition of calling the `LoadComponentData` function for each component type.

