Here's an example code that uses JavaScript to scroll to the top of the page when a button is clicked:

```javascript
function scrollToTop() {
  document.body.scrollTop = 0; // For Safari
  document.documentElement.scrollTop = 0; // For Chrome, Firefox, IE, and Opera
}

// Attach the function to a button element with an id of "scrollBtn"
var scrollBtn = document.getElementById("scrollBtn");
scrollBtn.addEventListener("click", scrollToTop);
```

When the `scrollToTop()` function is called, it sets the `scrollTop` property of both the `document.body` and the `document.documentElement` to `0`, which will effectively scroll the page to the top.

To trigger the `scrollToTop()` function when the button is clicked, we first need to select the button element using `document.getElementById()`. In this example, we're assuming that the button has an id of "scrollBtn". We then attach an event listener to the button using `addEventListener()` to listen for the "click" event. When the button is clicked, it will call the `scrollToTop()` function and scroll the page to the top.

Here's an example code that implements the described functionality in HTML5, CSS3, and JavaScript:

HTML code:
```HTML
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <style>
    /* Hide the button by default */
    #scrollBtn {
      display: none;
      opacity: 0;
      transition: opacity 0.5s ease-in-out;
    }

    /* Show the button when the user scrolls down 20px */
    #scrollBtn.visible {
      display: block;
      opacity: 1;
    }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
  <button id="scrollBtn">Scroll to Top</button>

  <script>
    /* Attach an event listener to the window to monitor scrolling */
    window.addEventListener('scroll', function() {
      var scrollBtn = document.getElementById('scrollBtn');
      if (window.pageYOffset > 20) { // if scrolled down at least 20 pixels
        scrollBtn.classList.add('visible');
      } else {
        scrollBtn.classList.remove('visible');
      }
    });

    /* Attach an event listener to the button to scroll to top */
    var scrollBtn = document.getElementById('scrollBtn');
    scrollBtn.addEventListener('click', function() {
      document.body.scrollTop = 0; // For Safari
      document.documentElement.scrollTop = 0; // For Chrome, Firefox, IE, and Opera
    });
  </script>
</body>

</html>
```

