In JUnit 5 with Spring Boot, `@ExtendWith(SpringExtension.class)` is used to enable Spring support for the test, but it is not sufficient to inject property values using the `@Value` annotation.

To use `@Value(${...})`, you also need to apply `@SpringBootTest` or `@ContextConfiguration` with the appropriate configuration classes or component scanning. `@SpringBootTest` is preferred for a Spring Boot application as it will load the full application context.

Here's a code example:

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.test.context.junit.jupiter.SpringExtension;

@ExtendWith(SpringExtension.class)
@SpringBootTest
public class YourTest {

    @Value("${your.property}")
    private String yourProperty;

    @Test
    public void testSomething() {
        // Do something with yourProperty
    }
}
```

Alternatively, you can use `@TestPropertySource`, if you don't want to load the whole Spring Boot application:

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.test.context.ContextConfiguration;
import org.springframework.test.context.TestPropertySource;
import org.springframework.test.context.junit.jupiter.SpringExtension;

@ExtendWith(SpringExtension.class)
@ContextConfiguration(classes = {YourTestConfiguration.class})
@TestPropertySource("classpath:test.properties")
public class YourTest {

    @Value("${your.property}")
    private String yourProperty;

    @Test
    public void testSomething() {
        // Do something with yourProperty
    }
}
```

In this case, create a `test.properties` file under `src/test/resources`, and place your property values there. Also, configure the `YourTestConfiguration` class with the required beans.

Either way, with `@SpringBootTest` or `@ContextConfiguration` in combination with `@ExtendWith(SpringExtension.class)`, you'll be able to inject property values using the `@Value` annotation.

