To increase the snake's length when it eats food, you can follow these steps:

1. Create an array to store the snake's body segments.

2. Initialize the snake with a head segment and a tail segment.

3. When the snake eats food, add a new body segment to the array at the end of the tail segment.

4. Update the tail segment's coordinates to be the same as the new body segment.

5. On each frame of the game loop, update the position of each body segment based on the position and direction of the previous segment. 

6. Draw each body segment on the canvas using its updated coordinates.

Here's an example of how you can modify your code to implement these steps:

```javascript
let snake = {
  body: [{x: 5, y: 5}, {x: 4, y: 5}], // start with head and tail
  direction: 'right',
  speed: 1
};

function eatFood() {
  const tail = snake.body[snake.body.length - 1];
  const newSegment = {x: tail.x, y: tail.y};
  snake.body.push(newSegment);
}

function updateSnakePosition() {
  // Move each body segment in the direction of the previous segment
  for (let i = snake.body.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    snake.body[i].x = snake.body[i - 1].x;
    snake.body[i].y = snake.body[i - 1].y;
  }

  // Move the head in the current direction
  const head = snake.body[0];
  if (snake.direction === 'up') {
    head.y -= snake.speed;
  } else if (snake.direction === 'down') {
    head.y += snake.speed;
  } else if (snake.direction === 'left') {
    head.x -= snake.speed;
  } else if (snake.direction === 'right') {
    head.x += snake.speed;
  }
}

function drawSnake() {
  const snakeColor = '#0f0';
  for (let segment of snake.body) {
    ctx.fillStyle = snakeColor;
    ctx.fillRect(segment.x * tileSize, segment.y * tileSize, tileSize, tileSize);
  }
}

function gameLoop() {
  updateSnakePosition();
  // Check for collision with food
  if (headCollidesWithFood()) {
    eatFood();
    // increase the speed or any other property you want as the snake grows
  }
  // Check for collision with walls or own body
  if (collisionDetected()) {
    endGame();
    return;
  }
  clearCanvas();
  drawFood();
  drawSnake();
  requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);
}
```

Here, we've added a `body` property to the `snake` object, which is an array of objects that represent each body segment. In the `eatFood()` function, we add a new segment to the end of the `body` array, which is a copy of the tail segment. In the `updateSnakePosition()` function, we move each segment in the direction of the previous segment, starting with the tail and ending with the head. In the `drawSnake()` function, we draw each body segment on the canvas using its updated coordinates. When the snake collides with food, we call the `eatFood()` function to add a new segment to the snake's body.

