#include <stdint.h>
#include "FreeRTOS.h"

typedef volatile struct
{
    uint32_t lock;
} Spinlock_t;

void spinlock_init(Spinlock_t *spinlock)
{
    spinlock->lock = 0;
}

void spinlock_acquire(Spinlock_t *spinlock)
{
    while (__sync_lock_test_and_set(&spinlock->lock, 1))
    {
        /* Spin and wait until the lock is released */
        taskYIELD();
    }
}

void spinlock_release(Spinlock_t *spinlock)
{
    __sync_lock_release(&spinlock->lock);
}
```

In this example, the `Spinlock_t` structure is defined to hold the `lock` variable as a 32-bit unsigned integer. The `spinlock_init` function is used to initialize the spinlock by setting the lock variable to 0.

The `spinlock_acquire` function is used to acquire the spinlock. It uses the `__sync_lock_test_and_set` function to set the lock variable to 1 atomically and check its previous value. If the previous value was 1, indicating that the lock was already acquired by another task, it enters a loop and repeatedly yields the processor using the `taskYIELD` function until the lock is released.

The `spinlock_release` function simply releases the spinlock by setting the lock variable to 0 using the `__sync_lock_release` function.

Please note that this example assumes that you have a working FreeRTOS setup and have included the necessary header files.

In Nginx, the concept of an "application pool" is not directly available as it is in IIS. However, you can achieve similar functionality by configuring Nginx to proxy requests to multiple backend servers or processes.

To create a setup similar to an application pool using Nginx, you can follow these steps:

1. Install and configure Nginx: Ensure that Nginx is installed on your server and properly configured. This typically involves editing the Nginx configuration file, which is usually located at `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf` or `/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf` on Linux systems.

2. Configure backend servers or processes: Set up your backend servers or processes that will serve your web application. These could be separate instances of your application running on different ports or servers.

3. Define upstream configuration: In the Nginx configuration file, define an "upstream" block that specifies the backend servers or processes to which Nginx will forward requests. Here's an example:

```
upstream my_app_backend {
    server 127.0.0.1:8000;
    server 127.0.0.1:8001;
    # Add more backend servers if needed
}
```

This configuration defines an upstream block named `my_app_backend` with two backend servers listening on `127.0.0.1:8000` and `127.0.0.1:8001`.

4. Configure server block: Create a server block in the Nginx configuration file that listens on the desired port and defines the proxy rules to forward requests to the backend servers. Here's an example:

```
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://my_app_backend;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    }
}
```

This configuration sets up Nginx to listen on port 80 for requests to `example.com`, and any request received is proxied to the backend servers defined in the upstream block (`my_app_backend`). The `proxy_set_header` lines are used to pass the original host and IP address headers to the backend servers if necessary.

5. Restart Nginx: Save the changes to the Nginx configuration file and restart Nginx to apply the new configuration.

After following these steps, Nginx will act as a reverse proxy and distribute incoming requests among the backend servers specified in the upstream block. This setup achieves similar functionality to an application pool by distributing the load across multiple backend servers or processes.

