In WPF, you can achieve this functionality by customizing the ComboBox with the help of ItemTemplate and DataTemplateSelector. You can create a custom control that extends ComboBox and add checkboxes inside the dropdown items.

Here's an example implementation:

1. Create a new WPF Custom Control - "CheckBoxComboBox" (inherited from ComboBox).
2. Create a class named "CheckBoxComboBoxItem" to hold the item data and selection state.
3. Create a custom DataTemplateSelector named "CheckBoxComboBoxItemTemplateSelector" that selects the appropriate DataTemplate for each item.
4. Implement the selection logic and update the ComboBox accordingly.

Here's a code snippet to help you get started:

```csharp
// Step 1: Create a Custom Control "CheckBoxComboBox"

public class CheckBoxComboBox : ComboBox
{
    static CheckBoxComboBox()
    {
        DefaultStyleKeyProperty.OverrideMetadata(typeof(CheckBoxComboBox), new FrameworkPropertyMetadata(typeof(CheckBoxComboBox)));
    }

    protected override void OnSelectionChanged(SelectionChangedEventArgs e)
    {
        base.OnSelectionChanged(e);

        foreach (object selectedItem in e.AddedItems)
        {
            if (selectedItem is CheckBoxComboBoxItem item)
            {
                item.IsChecked = true;
            }
        }

        foreach (object selectedItem in e.RemovedItems)
        {
            if (selectedItem is CheckBoxComboBoxItem item)
            {
                item.IsChecked = false;
            }
        }
    }
}


// Step 2: Create a class to hold item data and selection state

public class CheckBoxComboBoxItem : DependencyObject
{
    public static readonly DependencyProperty IsCheckedProperty = DependencyProperty.Register(
        "IsChecked", typeof(bool), typeof(CheckBoxComboBoxItem));

    public bool IsChecked
    {
        get { return (bool)GetValue(IsCheckedProperty); }
        set { SetValue(IsCheckedProperty, value); }
    }

    public object Content { get; set; }
}


// Step 3: Create a DataTemplateSelector to select templates

public class CheckBoxComboBoxItemTemplateSelector : DataTemplateSelector
{
    public DataTemplate ItemTemplate { get; set; }
    public DataTemplate SelectedItemTemplate { get; set; }

    public override DataTemplate SelectTemplate(object item, DependencyObject container)
    {
        if (container is FrameworkElement frameworkElement && item is CheckBoxComboBoxItem checkBoxComboBoxItem)
        {
            return checkBoxComboBoxItem.IsChecked ? SelectedItemTemplate : ItemTemplate;
        }

        return base.SelectTemplate(item, container);
    }
}
```

Now, you can use this custom control (CheckBoxComboBox) in XAML and define the ItemTemplate and SelectedItemTemplate as per your requirement:

```xml
<!-- In your XAML -->

<Grid>
    <local:CheckBoxComboBox Margin="10" Width="200">
        <local:CheckBoxComboBox.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate>
                <CheckBox Content="{Binding Content}" IsChecked="{Binding IsChecked, Mode=TwoWay}" />
            </DataTemplate>
        </local:CheckBoxComboBox.ItemTemplate>
        <local:CheckBoxComboBox.SelectedItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate>
                <CheckBox Content="{Binding Content}" IsChecked="{Binding IsChecked, Mode=TwoWay}" 
                            Foreground="Green" FontWeight="Bold" />
            </DataTemplate>
        </local:CheckBoxComboBox.SelectedItemTemplate>
        <local:CheckBoxComboBox.Items>
            <local:CheckBoxComboBoxItem Content="Item 1" />
            <local:CheckBoxComboBoxItem Content="Item 2" />
            <local:CheckBoxComboBoxItem Content="Item 3" />
        </local:CheckBoxComboBox.Items>
    </local:CheckBoxComboBox>
</Grid>
```

In this example, for every item in the ComboBox, a CheckBox is displayed. The item's selection state is bound to the CheckBox's IsChecked property. When an item is selected, it uses the SelectedItemTemplate; otherwise, it uses the ItemTemplate.

This implementation allows multiple checkboxes to be selected independently within the ComboBox.

