To create the desired functionality for a microbit using JavaScript, you can use the input from the accelerometer to detect the rotation angle of the microbit. When the angle exceeds 45 degrees, the microbit will play a beeping sound until it is turned back to the original angle. Here's an example code:

```javascript
let originalAngle = input.rotation(Dimension.Roll);

function playBeep() {
  music.playTone(Note.C, music.beat(BeatFraction.Half));
}

basic.forever(function () {
  let currentAngle = input.rotation(Dimension.Roll);

  // Check if the current angle exceeds 45 degrees
  if (currentAngle > originalAngle + 45 || currentAngle < originalAngle - 45) {
    playBeep();
  }
})
```

In this code, we first store the initial angle of the microbit in the variable `originalAngle` using `input.rotation(Dimension.Roll)`.

Then, inside the `basic.forever` loop, we continuously check the current angle using the same function. If the current angle exceeds 45 degrees from the original angle (in either direction), we call the `playBeep` function.

The `playBeep` function uses the `music` library provided by microbit to play a short beep sound. You can adjust the tone and duration of the beep by modifying the `playTone` arguments.

Remember to flash this code onto your microbit using the appropriate tool or editor.

