The function `init_waitqueue_head()` initializes a `struct wait_queue_head` data structure as a wait queue head. A wait queue head allows multiple processes to wait (block) for a specific condition to be satisfied before continuing execution. The `init_waitqueue_head()` function sets up the wait queue head by initializing the necessary fields for managing the queue.

The function prototype is as follows:
```c
void init_waitqueue_head(struct wait_queue_head *q);
```

The `struct wait_queue_head` data structure typically includes fields such as a lock to synchronize access, a list of queued waiters, and other fields specific to the implementation.

Example usage:
```c
#include <linux/wait.h>

struct wait_queue_head my_wait_queue;

int init_my_module(void)
{
    init_waitqueue_head(&my_wait_queue);
    // Rest of initialization logic...
}
```

In this example, `init_waitqueue_head()` is called to initialize the wait queue head `my_wait_queue` before using it in other parts of the module or program.

In FreeRTOS, there is no exact equivalent of `init_waitqueue_head()` function as FreeRTOS doesn't provide a built-in wait queue data structure like the one in Linux. However, you can achieve similar functionality by using a combination of task notifications and a task notification queue.

Here's a possible implementation of a wait queue head in FreeRTOS:

```c
#include "FreeRTOS.h"
#include "task.h"

typedef struct WaitQueueHead {
    TaskHandle_t taskHandle;
    QueueHandle_t notificationQueue;
} WaitQueueHead_t;

void init_waitqueue_head(WaitQueueHead_t *q) {
    q->taskHandle = xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle();
    q->notificationQueue = xQueueCreate(1, sizeof(uint32_t));
}

int wait_event_interruptible(WaitQueueHead_t *q, int condition) {
    int ret = pdFALSE;

    if (condition) {
        ret = xTaskNotifyWait(0, ULONG_MAX, NULL, portMAX_DELAY);
    }

    return ret == pdTRUE ? 0 : -1;
}

void wake_up(WaitQueueHead_t *q) {
    BaseType_t xHigherPriorityTaskWoken;
    xTaskNotifyGive(q->taskHandle);
    xQueueSendFromISR(q->notificationQueue, NULL, &xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);
    portYIELD_FROM_ISR(xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);
}
```

In this implementation, `WaitQueueHead_t` struct holds the task handle and the notification queue associated with the wait queue head. `init_waitqueue_head()` initializes the wait queue head by setting the current task handle and creating a notification queue.

The `wait_event_interruptible()` function waits until the specified condition is met. If the condition is true, it calls `xTaskNotifyWait()` to suspend the task until it receives a notification.

The `wake_up()` function wakes up the task associated with the wait queue head by giving a notification using `xTaskNotifyGive()` and sending a message to the notification queue.

Note that this implementation assumes that there is only one task waiting on the wait queue head. If you need to support multiple tasks waiting, you can modify the implementation to use a linked list of task handles or other appropriate data structure.

To support multiple tasks waiting on the wait queue head in FreeRTOS, you can modify the implementation to use a linked list of task handles. Here's an updated version:

```c
#include "FreeRTOS.h"
#include "task.h"

typedef struct WaitQueueHead {
    struct TaskListNode* taskList;
    QueueHandle_t notificationQueue;
} WaitQueueHead_t;

typedef struct TaskListNode {
    TaskHandle_t taskHandle;
    struct TaskListNode* next;
} TaskListNode_t;

void init_waitqueue_head(WaitQueueHead_t *q) {
    q->taskList = NULL;
    q->notificationQueue = xQueueCreate(1, sizeof(uint32_t));
}

int wait_event_interruptible(WaitQueueHead_t *q, int condition) {
    int ret = pdFALSE;

    if (condition) {
        TaskListNode_t node;
        node.taskHandle = xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle();
        node.next = NULL;

        xQueueReset(q->notificationQueue);

        // Add the current task to the task list
        if (q->taskList == NULL) {
            q->taskList = &node;
        } else {
            TaskListNode_t* curr = q->taskList;
            while (curr->next != NULL) {
                curr = curr->next;
            }
            curr->next = &node;
        }

        // Wait for a notification
        ret = xTaskNotifyWait(0, ULONG_MAX, NULL, portMAX_DELAY);

        // Remove the current task from the task list
        if (q->taskList == &node) {
            q->taskList = node.next;
        } else {
            TaskListNode_t* curr = q->taskList;
            while (curr->next != &node) {
                curr = curr->next;
            }
            curr->next = node.next;
        }
    }

    return ret == pdTRUE ? 0 : -1;
}

void wake_up_all(WaitQueueHead_t *q) {
    BaseType_t xHigherPriorityTaskWoken;
    xTaskNotifyGiveFromISR(q->taskList->taskHandle, &xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);
    xQueueSendFromISR(q->notificationQueue, NULL, &xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);
    portYIELD_FROM_ISR(xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);
}
```

In this updated implementation, the `WaitQueueHead` struct now includes a `taskList` field, which is a linked list of `TaskListNode` structs. Each `TaskListNode` holds a task handle and a pointer to the next node in the list.

The `wait_event_interruptible` function has been modified to add the current task to the task list when waiting and remove it when finished waiting. This allows multiple tasks to wait on the same wait queue head.

The `wake_up_all` function has been modified to give a notification to each task in the task list using `xTaskNotifyGiveFromISR`.

Note that in this implementation, `wake_up_all` is used to wake up all waiting tasks. If you only want to wake up one waiting task, you can modify the implementation to use `wake_up` function which wakes up only the task at the head of the task list.

