Creating a 2D double array with dimensions 10000 by 10000 in C++ may result in a significant amount of memory allocation. It is important to consider memory limitations before attempting such an allocation. Here's an example of how you can do it:

```c++
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    // Define array dimensions
    const int rows = 10000;
    const int cols = 10000;

    // Create the 2D double array
    double** array = new double*[rows];
    for (int i = 0; i < rows; ++i) {
        array[i] = new double[cols];
    }

    // Access and modify array elements
    array[0][0] = 5.7;
    array[rows-1][cols-1] = 9.2;

    // Delete the array to free memory
    for (int i = 0; i < rows; ++i) {
        delete[] array[i];
    }
    delete[] array;

    return 0;
}
```

Keep in mind that allocating such a large 2D array may cause memory allocation errors if there isn't enough memory available. It is advisable to consider alternative solutions or optimize the code if memory limitations become an issue.

