One possible solution to avoid the double free issue in your copy constructor is to perform a deep copy of the file buffer structure. This way, you create a new copy of the pointer and allocate memory for it separately, instead of just copying the pointer itself.

Here's an example implementation of a copy constructor that performs a deep copy:

```cpp
class File {
private:
    char* buffer;
public:
    // Copy constructor
    File(const File& other) {
        // Allocate memory for the new buffer
        buffer = new char[strlen(other.buffer) + 1];
        // Copy the contents of the other buffer to the new buffer
        strcpy(buffer, other.buffer);
    }
};
```

In this code, when the copy constructor is called, it allocates memory for the new buffer using the `new` keyword. Then, it copies the contents of the other buffer to the new buffer using a function like `strcpy()`.

By performing a deep copy like this, each object will have its own separate buffer, preventing the double free issue when one of the objects is destroyed.

Remember to implement a destructor to properly free the dynamically allocated memory when the object is destroyed:

```cpp
class File {
private:
    char* buffer;
public:
    // Constructor
    File(const char* content) {
        buffer = new char[strlen(content) + 1];
        strcpy(buffer, content);
    }

    // Copy constructor
    File(const File& other) {
        buffer = new char[strlen(other.buffer) + 1];
        strcpy(buffer, other.buffer);
    }

    // Destructor
    ~File() {
        delete[] buffer;
    }
};
```

By implementing the destructor with `delete[] buffer`, the dynamically allocated memory for each buffer will be properly freed when the object is destroyed.

