To check if an `std::unordered_map` in C++ contains a specific key, you can use the `count()` function or the `find()` function. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>

int main() {
    std::unordered_map<int, std::string> names;

    // Insert some key-value pairs
    names.insert({1, "Alice"});
    names.insert({2, "Bob"});
    names.insert({3, "Charlie"});

    // Check if the map contains a key
    int key = 2;
    if (names.count(key) > 0) {
        std::cout << "The key " << key << " is present in the unordered_map." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "The key " << key << " is not present in the unordered_map." << std::endl;
    }

    // Alternative approach using find()
    if (names.find(key) != names.end()) {
        std::cout << "The key " << key << " is present in the unordered_map." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "The key " << key << " is not present in the unordered_map." << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we create an `std::unordered_map` called `names`. We insert some key-value pairs into the map, and then check if a specific key (2 in this case) is present using the `count()` function or the `find()` function. If the count is greater than 0, it means the key exists in the map. If `find()` returns an iterator not equal to `end()`, it means the key exists in the map.

To check if a given set of points in an XY plane form a straight line, you can calculate the slope between the first two points, then compare it with the slopes between the remaining points. If all slopes are equal, the points lie on a straight line.

Here's an example implementation in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// Calculate the slope between two points
double calculateSlope(const std::vector<int>& p1, const std::vector<int>& p2) {
    // Avoid division by zero if the points are on the vertical line
    if (p1[0] == p2[0]) {
        return std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
    }

    return static_cast<double>(p2[1] - p1[1]) / (p2[0] - p1[0]);
}

bool checkStraightLine(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& coordinates) {
    // Get the first two points to calculate the initial slope
    const std::vector<int>& p1 = coordinates[0];
    const std::vector<int>& p2 = coordinates[1];
    double slope = calculateSlope(p1, p2);

    // Check the slope with all other points
    for (int i = 2; i < coordinates.size(); i++) {
        const std::vector<int>& start = coordinates[i - 1];
        const std::vector<int>& end = coordinates[i];
        double currSlope = calculateSlope(start, end);

        // If any slope is not equal to the initial slope, return false
        if (currSlope != slope) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    return true;
}

int main() {
    // Example usage
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> coordinates = {{1, 2}, {2, 3}, {3, 4}, {4, 5}};
    bool isStraight = checkStraightLine(coordinates);

    std::cout << "The points ";
    for (const auto& point : coordinates) {
        std::cout << "[" << point[0] << ", " << point[1] << "] ";
    }
    std::cout << "form a straight line: " << std::boolalpha << isStraight << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we define a function `checkStraightLine()` that takes a vector of vectors `coordinates` as input. It calculates the slope between the first two points and then compares it with the slopes between all subsequent points in the loop.

We also define a helper function `calculateSlope()` to compute the slope between two points, taking care of the vertical line case where the slope is infinity.

Finally, we test the function using the example coordinates `{1, 2}, {2, 3}, {3, 4}, {4, 5}` and print the result. If the points form a straight line, the output will be "The points [1, 2] [2, 3] [3, 4] [4, 5] form a straight line: true".

To start a timer in Linux, you can make use of the POSIX timer APIs. The specific API to call is `timer_create()`. Here's a basic example of how to start a timer using the POSIX timer APIs:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <time.h>

// Timer callback function
void timer_callback(union sigval sv) {
    printf("Timer expired!\n");
}

int main() {
    struct sigevent sev;
    timer_t timerid;
    struct itimerspec its;

    // Set up the timer callback function
    sev.sigev_notify = SIGEV_THREAD;
    sev.sigev_notify_function = timer_callback;
    sev.sigev_value.sival_ptr = &timerid;

    // Create the timer
    if (timer_create(CLOCK_REALTIME, &sev, &timerid) == -1) {
        perror("timer_create");
        return 1;
    }

    // Set the timer interval (here, 5 seconds)
    its.it_interval.tv_sec = 5;
    its.it_interval.tv_nsec = 0;

    // Set the initial expiration time (here, 2 seconds from now)
    its.it_value.tv_sec = 2;
    its.it_value.tv_nsec = 0;

    // Start the timer
    if (timer_settime(timerid, 0, &its, NULL) == -1) {
        perror("timer_settime");
        return 1;
    }

    // Keep the program running to observe timer callbacks
    while (1) {
        usleep(1000);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The above code sets up a timer that triggers a callback function every 5 seconds. The initial expiration time is set to 2 seconds from when the timer is started. You can modify the interval and initial expiration as per your requirements.

Compile the code using `gcc` and run the generated binary to observe the timer expiring and the callback function being called.
